WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:02.347
- [Helen] This video is
brought to you by Raycon.

00:00:02.347 --> 00:00:04.847
(happy music)

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:08.780
- What's up, everybody, Helen here.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:11.330
It's a pretty good time
to be an adult cartoon.

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:12.990
From the swear-happy South Park.

00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.370
- Hey, (beep), where's
my mutha (beep) money?

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.560
- [Helen] To the constant
flashbacking of Family Guy

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:20.450
- And those guys back at
the factory can suck it.

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:22.360
I'm gonna get outta this place one day.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.540
- [Helen] To the liquor-fueled
antics of Archer and Co.,

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.470
many animated shows for grown-ass folks

00:00:26.470 --> 00:00:29.240
have become enduring
21st Century classics.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.480
But we'd argue that

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.060
possibly the most adult
cartoon of them all

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.740
is one, BoJack Horseman.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:36.380
It's not as profane as South Park,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.060
nor as sexually charged as Archer.

00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.110
- Because I've swallowed
just about as much

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:41.210
as I can take from you!

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.610
- Hey, phrasing!

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:44.480
- [Helen] But in terms of
catapulting it's audience

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:46.400
into a befuddled cloud angsty depression,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:47.800
the simultaneously hilarious

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.950
and gut-wrenching world of
Hollywoo never fails to deliver.

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:52.078
- Look, I gotta go.

00:00:52.078 --> 00:00:53.900
- I feel like you didn't
really finish the pep talk.

00:00:53.900 --> 00:00:57.220
- I know, but I got a movie
to put together, best of luck.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:59.030
- [Helen] Part of what
makes BoJack so beloved

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:00.940
is the way it defies convention.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.880
And not just in regards to the ethics of

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.330
inter-species romantic love.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:05.970
The show features frequently depressed

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.240
and f'd up characters,

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.660
frequently doing pretty
depressing and f'd up things.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:11.255
Particularly in its later seasons,

00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:13.500
the show is less about the lovable antics

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.100
of your favorite on-screen pals

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.980
than it is an emotionally-trying look

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:19.817
into the lives of severely
flawed and unhappy people.

00:01:19.817 --> 00:01:22.930
- Now we just have to solve
the drug crisis in America.

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:24.440
- Easy! We'll take them all.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:25.360
- Yes!

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.590
- [Helen] The most recent half-season

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:28.910
seems to depict an
evolving version of BoJack

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.330
fighting his addictions and
becoming a better horse,

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:33.390
along with some smaller, also promising

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.800
character arcs for his friends.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.080
But now that the show is
coming to a conclusion,

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.700
it has to wrestle with its
fundamental underpinning.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:41.080
Despite their many attempts,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.180
the characters don't really change.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.370
Understanding why the characters
on BoJack are so static

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:48.560
requires examining the
greater conversation

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:51.240
the show is having with a
familiar form of entertainment.

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:52.580
The comedic sitcom.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.670
We'll explain in this Wisecrack edition on

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:56.590
Change in BoJack Horseman.

