00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.640
Now, let's dive into a
recap of Season 6 so far,

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:20.140
which finds BoJack enduring,

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:22.200
and even semi-thriving in rehab,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:23.310
where he waters plants,

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:25.630
accidentally gets his
sober horse-doctor drunk,

00:03:25.630 --> 00:03:27.440
and speaks unnecessary truths

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.900
to the not-powerful
recovering addicts around him.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:32.560
- Really. Everyone's being honest?

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:34.700
Doug, over here, who keeps
dressing in a suit and tie

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.356
even though he's never
getting his finance job back.

00:03:36.356 --> 00:03:37.189
(gasps)

00:03:37.189 --> 00:03:39.043
- [Helen] Eventually forced
to return to real life,

00:03:39.043 --> 00:03:40.670
BoJack finds himself haunted

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:43.690
by the morally bankrupt
horse he used to be.

00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:45.990
He embarks on a cross-country
trip to make amends

00:03:45.990 --> 00:03:47.660
and figure out what to do with himself,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.320
finally settling, it
seems, on teaching acting

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:53.080
at the hipster-studded liberal
arts college, Wesleyan,

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:55.190
where his half-sister,
Hollyhock, is studying.

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:56.850
However, just when it seems like

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.960
BoJack is really becoming a better version

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.060
of that vodka-soaked,
self-obsessed celebrity

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:03.090
we've come to know and
yell at in our heads,

00:04:03.090 --> 00:04:04.530
something else happens.

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.700
Specifically, the eponymous
character doesn't appear at all

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:09.650
in the final episode of this half-season.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.880
Instead, the show focuses entirely

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.020
on the victims of his toxic behavior.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.870
From Sarah Lynn, who is super-dead

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:18.390
under mysterious circumstances
currently being investigated

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:19.810
by two hard-nosed reporters,

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.560
to Gina, who still has PTSD from the time

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:25.110
BoJack got too into character
and straight up choked her,

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.940
to Kelsey, whose career is still suffering

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:29.370
thanks to BoJack's antics.

00:04:29.370 --> 00:04:30.523
- If you told me when we were starting out

00:04:30.523 --> 00:04:32.690
that one day you'd be
doing chicken commercials--

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:33.540
- Not a commercial.

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.470
- [Helen] Worst of all,
this half of the season ends

00:04:35.470 --> 00:04:37.100
with Hollyhock just on the precipice

00:04:37.100 --> 00:04:38.850
of discovering a horrible truth.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:39.940
- Who is he?

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:41.810
- [Helen] That when
BoJack was in New Mexico,

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:43.990
he took his ex's daughter, Penny, to prom,

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:47.090
got her friends wasted, and
almost hooked up with her.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:47.923
Woof.

00:04:47.923 --> 00:04:49.570
So, what is the series setting up for

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:50.950
in the final eight episodes?

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:53.360
And what issues should you
expect the show to grapple with?

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:54.420
We have an idea.

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.380
But first, we have to talk
about the Big Bang Theory.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:04:58.720
It's relevant, we swear.

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:01.387
Now, when Greek philosopher,
Heraclitus of Ephesus, said,

00:05:01.387 --> 00:05:03.290
"Change is the only constant in life,"

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:05.770
he clearly couldn't have
predicted the television sitcom.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:07.890
The sitcom, or a typically family-friendly

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.340
comedic television show,

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:11.660
is fundamentally rooted in its characters.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.360
And those characters are generally
rooted in predictability.

00:05:14.360 --> 00:05:17.467
As scholar Jason Mittell
argues in his book, Complex TV,

00:05:17.467 --> 00:05:19.797
"While it may seem that a
pleasure of serial narratives

00:05:19.797 --> 00:05:22.377
"is watching characters
grow and develop over time,

00:05:22.377 --> 00:05:25.017
"most television characters
are more stable and consistent

00:05:25.017 --> 00:05:26.430
"rather than changeable entities."

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:29.240
He notes that, even in the
face of life-altering events,

00:05:29.240 --> 00:05:31.589
characters are more likely
to accumulate experiences

00:05:31.589 --> 00:05:34.610
rather to actually learn
and change from them,

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:36.520
the way your Life Coach
suggests you should.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:38.890
We see this all throughout
the history of sitcoms.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:40.970
From the self-absorbed Major Frank Burns

00:05:40.970 --> 00:05:42.330
on the 70's show, Mash,

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.090
to the goofy antics of Kramer,

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.500
to the even goofier
antics of Michael Scott.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.559
- Hey, Michael, did you
fall into a koi pond?

00:05:48.559 --> 00:05:50.400
- Ah, I can't really hear you.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:52.530
I think we have sort of a bad connection.

00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:54.690
- [Helen] As much as we love
the Sopranos and Mad Men

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:56.470
for their psychological complexity

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.520
and deep, ever-evolving characters,

00:05:58.520 --> 00:05:59.600
we love plenty of shows

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:01.700
precisely for their
lack of these qualities.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.610
It's because Friends is not the Sopranos

00:06:03.610 --> 00:06:05.820
that we want to watch it
at 2pm when we're hungover

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.570
or 2am when we can't sleep.

00:06:07.570 --> 00:06:09.010
- My life is an embarrassment,

00:06:09.010 --> 00:06:10.933
I should just go live
under somebody's stairs.

00:06:10.933 --> 00:06:11.766
(laughter)

00:06:11.766 --> 00:06:14.040
- [Helen] Minor changes might
occur episode to episode

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.220
as the over-arching
plots are driven forward,

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.240
the shows themselves remain
fundamentally static.

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.510
Marriages, promotions
and other developments

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:22.660
are only skin-deep.

00:06:22.660 --> 00:06:24.480
The relationships between the central cast

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:27.120
don't really change, nor do
the characters themselves.

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.120
This structure makes them easy to consume.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:32.380
You can dip in at any point and
know exactly what to expect.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:35.520
But, more importantly, it blesses
them with the promise that

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:38.200
whatever happens, everything
will have gone back to normal

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:39.276
by the end of the episode.

00:06:39.276 --> 00:06:40.109
- Ahhhhhh!

00:06:40.109 --> 00:06:41.580
(laughter)

00:06:41.580 --> 00:06:44.140
- [Helen] Comfortable,
familiar, always the same.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.140
Usually set in cozy houses, cafes or bars,

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:49.810
these shows keep us nestled
in a comfy status quo

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:51.747
that makes the bitter difficulties
of life feel far away,

00:06:51.747 --> 00:06:54.110
for at least 20 minutes or so.

00:06:54.110 --> 00:06:56.500
Imagine if Homer Simpson
suddenly didn't want a bite

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:57.760
of that jelly-filled doughnut.

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:00.300
You would probably,
understandably, spiral.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.990
What makes BoJack Horseman
so heart-breaking to watch

00:07:02.990 --> 00:07:05.620
is that while the show
maintains the sitcom format

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:06.670
by keeping its characters

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:08.610
on a perpetual emotional hamster wheel,

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:11.270
those characters actually
seem to want to change.

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.950
And their inability to do so

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:15.260
is the crux of the
show's painful resonance.

00:07:15.260 --> 00:07:17.470
The perfect foil for the
show, BoJack Horseman,

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.860
is its delightful meta
show, Horsin' Around,

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.170
a series best-described by BoJack himself.

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:23.270
- Look, for a lot of people

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:26.420
life is just one lone,
hard kick in the urethra.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:28.480
And sometimes, when you
get home from a long day

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:29.620
of getting kicked in the urethra,

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:31.860
you just wanna watch a show about good,

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:33.730
likable people who love each other.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:35.570
Where, you know, no matter what happens,

00:07:35.570 --> 00:07:39.150
at the end of 30 minutes,
everything's gonna turn out okay.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.500
- [Helen] Key to the turning
out okay part is that,

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:43.950
like most sitcoms, nothing really changes.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:45.340
The characters are monolithic,

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.810
simply drawn, and reliably themselves.

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:50.510
In this 90's comedy
evocative of the Cosby Show,

00:07:50.510 --> 00:07:52.790
BoJack played the bubbly,
gold-hearted father-figure

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:54.340
to three adorable orphans,

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.960
reciting a cheesy platitude
at the end of each episode.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.120
- We're a family now, no matter what,

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.510
and I will always be there for you.

00:08:01.510 --> 00:08:03.620
- [Helen] The show's
sugary tone of broad humor

00:08:03.620 --> 00:08:06.380
and familiar rhythms made
BoJack rich and famous.

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:09.440
But, even 30 years later, he's
still haunted by the question

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:10.890
or whether it was any good.

00:08:10.890 --> 00:08:13.980
Still, throughout the series,
BoJack has sought solace

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:15.800
and comfort in Horsin' Around reruns,

00:08:15.800 --> 00:08:18.540
which he consumes with an
enthusiasm he typically reserves

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:20.020
only for horse tranquilizers.

00:08:20.020 --> 00:08:22.570
Reciting lines, reminiscing over bad jokes

00:08:22.570 --> 00:08:24.233
- Nay way, Jose.

00:08:25.383 --> 00:08:26.748
(laughs)

00:08:26.748 --> 00:08:28.036
- Nay way, Jose.

00:08:28.036 --> 00:08:29.100
(laughs)

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:31.180
- [Helen] And generally
reflecting on what seems to be

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:32.810
the best era of his life.

00:08:32.810 --> 00:08:34.740
He appears to long for the simple,

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.760
easy emotional connections experienced

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:39.640
by the horse he sees on TV,
even attempting to assume

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:41.720
a good-natured fatherly
role with Sarah Lynn

00:08:41.720 --> 00:08:43.920
when she briefly moves
in with him in Season 1,

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.890
to disastrously,
metaphorically-incestuous results.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.750
We can pretty much all
relate to BoJack's sentiment.

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:52.030
Life would be easier if
we lived on a static,

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.300
happy-family TV show

00:08:53.300 --> 00:08:55.310
where everything is
relatively predictable.

00:08:55.310 --> 00:08:57.849
But for him, since he
literally was on one,

00:08:57.849 --> 00:09:00.690
the contrast becomes all
the more heart-breaking.

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:02.900
Horsin' Around is often
explicitly contrasted

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:04.270
with BoJack's real life,

00:09:04.270 --> 00:09:06.095
and that's where things
get really interesting.

00:09:06.095 --> 00:09:08.620
Having lived the most significant
years of his existence

00:09:08.620 --> 00:09:11.320
on on a sitcom set, BoJack
has come to understand

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:13.980
the utter vapidness of
this inert sitcom world.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:15.370
- The most important thing is

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:17.420
you gotta give the people what they want.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:18.570
Even if it kills you,

00:09:18.570 --> 00:09:19.780
even if it empties you out

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:21.670
until there's nothing left to empty.

00:09:21.670 --> 00:09:23.740
- [Helen] As much as he
longs to return endlessly

00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.670
to the place in which
nothing ever changes,

00:09:25.670 --> 00:09:28.100
he is tormented by the fact that this is,

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:31.780
ironically and tragically,
exactly what his life has become.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:34.580
He's become a piece of
(beep) who never changes.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:37.270
Or, perhaps more
precisely, BoJack is living

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:40.210
in an existentially
dreadful shadow of a sitcom.

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:41.250
We can make out as much

00:09:41.250 --> 00:09:42.840
from the opening credits of the show,

00:09:42.840 --> 00:09:46.540
which, until Season 6, depict
BoJack rising from his bed

00:09:46.540 --> 00:09:47.850
in a half-drugged haze.

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.009
As he floats, a series
of background images

00:09:50.009 --> 00:09:51.910
slowly flash behind him.

00:09:51.910 --> 00:09:53.960
The backgrounds change
from season to season,

00:09:53.960 --> 00:09:56.540
but the spaced-out,
unreal quality remains,

00:09:56.540 --> 00:09:58.750
hinting that he might
continue on this carousel

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:00.040
until the very end.

00:10:00.040 --> 00:10:02.310
That's what the characters
in BoJack Horseman do.

00:10:02.310 --> 00:10:05.370
They go round and round in
the same interior tracks,

00:10:05.370 --> 00:10:07.290
always ending back where they started.

00:10:07.290 --> 00:10:09.900
Much like the characters on
Full House or The Brady Bunch,

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:12.510
they same to be fated
to never really change.

00:10:12.510 --> 00:10:14.783
- I told you to wait in the car!

00:10:15.624 --> 00:10:16.600
(bottle smash)

00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.810
- [Helen] Despite trying
really, really hard to.

00:10:18.810 --> 00:10:20.560
The prime example is BoJack,

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:22.940
who in a nature befitting
a suffering addict,

00:10:22.940 --> 00:10:25.580
professes to want to transform
himself and his life,

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:27.230
but never quite succeeds.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:28.600
In the second season,

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:31.860
BoJack makes a conscious attempt
to overhaul his personality

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:33.100
by plugging into a series

00:10:33.100 --> 00:10:35.410
of chipper, metaphor-mixing
self-help tapes.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:36.390
They don't work because

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.020
they're attempts at instantaneous change,

00:10:38.020 --> 00:10:40.010
only really served to tinker about

00:10:40.010 --> 00:10:41.390
on the surface of a person.

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:44.700
A smile painted sloppily on the
outermost layer of the self.

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:47.570
He takes up running as part
of his self-improvement quest,

00:10:47.570 --> 00:10:48.840
but rarely gets far.

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:50.950
At the end of that season's final episode,

00:10:50.950 --> 00:10:53.370
he takes to the hill
outside his home once again.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:56.760
He grunts, complains and
collapses almost immediately,

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.708
only to be roused by a voice above him.

00:10:58.708 --> 00:11:00.540
- [Macaque] It gets easier.

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:01.373
- Huh?

00:11:01.373 --> 00:11:03.100
- [Helen] It's the macaque
who has been quietly jogging

00:11:03.100 --> 00:11:04.071
through each episode.

00:11:04.071 --> 00:11:06.223
- Every day it gets a little easier.

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:08.103
- Yeah?

00:11:08.103 --> 00:11:11.410
- But you've gotta do it every
day. That's the hard part.

00:11:11.410 --> 00:11:12.780
But it does get easier.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:14.510
- Here, the primate seems to voice

00:11:14.510 --> 00:11:16.280
the show's perspective on change.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:18.020
That it's hard and life will kick your ass

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:20.140
as you try to shift your
ingrained behaviors,

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:23.100
but that even the smallest
change can be super rewarding.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:25.560
Lesson learned, right? Not so fast.

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:29.010
Season 3 returns only to
watch BoJack behave selfishly,

00:11:29.010 --> 00:11:30.780
i.e., sleeping with Todd's ex, Emily,

00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:32.350
succumb to his addictions,

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:35.240
i.e., going on a pre-AA
bender with Sarah Lynn,

00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:37.540
and feel depressed and isolated,

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:40.900
visa vie this incredible
dialogue-free underwater episode.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.910
His inability to change is
perhaps satirized in the episode,

00:11:43.910 --> 00:11:46.970
Stop the Presses, in which
BoJack has to leap through hoops

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:49.300
in an attempt to cancel
a newspaper subscription.

00:11:49.300 --> 00:11:52.050
As if driving home the point
that, in this universe,

00:11:52.050 --> 00:11:54.460
even the most minute,
bureaucratic alteration

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:56.760
is impossible with a capital I-M.

00:11:56.760 --> 00:11:59.570
But arguably, the biggest
joke in quotation marks

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:01.170
comes at the end of the season.

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:02.810
BoJack attempts to commit suicide

00:12:02.810 --> 00:12:04.710
by forcing his car to crash,

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:06.550
only to change his mind last-minute,

00:12:06.550 --> 00:12:08.960
and is promptly rewarded
with the majestic site

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:11.560
of a pack of horses running wild and free.

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:13.441
That's right, he's exactly where he was

00:12:13.441 --> 00:12:16.680
at the end of Season 2,
wishing he could run.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:18.920
This continues for the
next couple of seasons

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:20.900
as BoJack indulges in his vices

00:12:20.900 --> 00:12:22.620
and hurts the people he loves,

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:24.610
eventually contributing
to Sarah Lynn's death

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:26.170
in the planetarium after going

00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:28.440
on a days' long epic bender with her.

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:29.440
- Right, Sarah Lynn?

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:32.393
Sarah Lynn?

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:36.170
- [Helen] After her funeral,
BoJack reflects on his toxicity

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.380
and in one rather on-the-nose moment,

00:12:38.380 --> 00:12:40.540
seems to state the show's apparent thesis.

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:42.140
- I don't know how to be, Diane.

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:44.280
Doesn't get better and
it doesn't get easier.

00:12:44.280 --> 00:12:46.630
I can't lying to myself
saying that I'm gonna change,

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:47.490
I'm poison.

00:12:47.490 --> 00:12:49.170
- [Helen] So much for self-improvement.

00:12:49.170 --> 00:12:51.690
Even when you think BoJack could
be getting slightly better,

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:54.560
say, thanks to the influence
of his half-sister, Hollyhock,

00:12:54.560 --> 00:12:56.100
he'll be back with a fifth season

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.230
centered around his new TV show, Philbert,

00:12:58.230 --> 00:12:59.780
in which he'll completely devolve

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:01.480
into an opiate-fueled terror.

00:13:01.480 --> 00:13:03.370
The other characters
don't fair much better,

00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:05.180
at least in terms of personal development.

00:13:05.180 --> 00:13:07.530
Princess Carolyn struggles
with her addiction to work,

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:09.450
difficulty maintaining a relationship,

00:13:09.450 --> 00:13:11.520
and then over her desire for a child.

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:14.150
And then her ambivalence
about having a child.

00:13:14.150 --> 00:13:17.290
Regardless of the scenario,
she both feels overwhelmed

00:13:17.290 --> 00:13:19.240
and doesn't feel like she's doing enough.

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:21.240
She goes from agent to manager,

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:22.910
from girlfriend to single-mom,

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:25.010
from ruthless to empathetic,

00:13:25.010 --> 00:13:27.100
but never really changes.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.380
Meanwhile, Diane agonizes
over her integrity.

00:13:30.380 --> 00:13:33.320
Whether it's the societal
impact of her girlcroosh videos,

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:35.870
or her role in enabling
BoJack's bad behavior.

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:37.320
The sense that her work and her life

00:13:37.320 --> 00:13:39.310
should mean more always haunts her.

00:13:39.310 --> 00:13:42.270
She seeks to express herself
creatively with integrity

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:45.760
in a refugee camp, or in
Vietnam, or in writing a memoir.

00:13:45.760 --> 00:13:47.450
And yet, each season,

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:50.480
the characters wind up pretty
much back where they began.

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:51.900
Todd is still a man-child,

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:55.200
Mr. Peanutbutter still lives
in mostly blissful ignorance,

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.580
and BoJack, even 30 years later,

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:00.305
is still the derelict
horse from Horsin' Around.

00:14:00.305 --> 00:14:02.330
(drink spilling)

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:03.250
- When!

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:05.400
- [Helen] Unlike your Cheers!
or I Love Lucy's, though,

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:07.800
there is no comfort in this static state.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.310
There is no peace in
BoJack's tragic flaws.

00:14:10.310 --> 00:14:12.100
Because BoJack Horseman shines a light

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.860
on the dark crevasses of humanity,

00:14:13.860 --> 00:14:16.560
or horse-manity, that sitcoms ignore.

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:20.190
It's characters are simultaneously
deeper and more tragic

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:21.740
than your typical Frasier affair.

00:14:21.740 --> 00:14:23.920
The repetitive nature of
each character's story

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:26.920
feels less like the fun-loving
antics of All In The Family

00:14:26.920 --> 00:14:28.940
and more like the actual living-nightmare

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:31.550
of a human being trying
to change in real life.

00:14:31.550 --> 00:14:33.662
- That's too much, man!

00:14:33.662 --> 00:14:35.060
(laughter)

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:37.310
- The unchanging nature
of things in Hollywoo

00:14:37.310 --> 00:14:39.810
becomes a torturous existential trap,

00:14:39.810 --> 00:14:42.380
a bitter and continual
reminder of the fact that

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:44.210
real life is kina the same.

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:45.620
No matter what happens today,

00:14:45.620 --> 00:14:47.640
you'll wake up the same person tomorrow.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:50.690
No promotion, no marriage,
no big symbolic change

00:14:50.690 --> 00:14:52.500
will take you away from who you are.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:55.070
So, BoJack's effectively stuck, right?

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:56.050
Not quite.

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:57.890
The show isn't simply content to teach us

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.290
that people can't change no
matter how hard they try,

00:15:00.290 --> 00:15:03.420
Season 6 thus far feels
more devastating than that.

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:05.770
Rather than dark sitcom-like repetition,

00:15:05.770 --> 00:15:07.720
the eight episodes witness BoJack

00:15:07.720 --> 00:15:10.320
actually seeming to evolve
in a meaningful way,

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:11.600
albeit slowly and painfully.

00:15:11.600 --> 00:15:14.910
Still imperfect, he enables
teenage rehab resident Jameson

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:17.880
to behave badly and act out,
much as he did with Sarah Lynn.

00:15:17.880 --> 00:15:20.430
But, even when he messes up, he's trying.

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:23.140
Yes, he keeps vodka around
because he thinks he's special

00:15:23.140 --> 00:15:24.630
and can withstand his addiction.

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:27.350
And yes, that vodka accidentally
gets doctor horse drunk.

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:29.120
But he stays with said horse doctor

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:31.300
and repeatedly tries to
get him back on the wagon.

00:15:31.300 --> 00:15:32.650
You know, progress.

00:15:32.650 --> 00:15:34.630
Plus, he urges Diane to get on meds,

00:15:34.630 --> 00:15:36.760
tries to find Todd an asexual partner,

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:39.460
and he even brings Princess
Carolyn's baby a present.

00:15:39.460 --> 00:15:41.510
Though, it naturally is
a portrait of himself

00:15:41.510 --> 00:15:42.380
as a narcissist.

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:45.550
Hell, he even gives Mr.
Peanutbutter a crossover episode.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:47.177
And by the end of the eight episode,

00:15:47.177 --> 00:15:49.840
other characters seem to be
making small changes, too.

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:51.550
Diane goes on antidepressants,

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:53.990
Princess Carolyn seems
to reset her priorities,

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.320
Todd realizes his
perpetual childlike nature

00:15:56.320 --> 00:15:57.620
makes him an excellent manny,

00:15:57.620 --> 00:16:00.370
and Mr. Peanutbutter attempts
to atone for his infidelity.

00:16:00.370 --> 00:16:03.130
These changes are small,
maybe even microscopic,

00:16:03.130 --> 00:16:04.330
but they're there.

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:06.590
Every inch of change has been hard-won.

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:08.730
Is something meaningful happening? Maybe.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:11.020
And it feels most profound in episode 7,

00:16:11.020 --> 00:16:13.580
when BoJack travels the country
visiting some of the people

00:16:13.580 --> 00:16:15.310
he's hurt or alienated.

00:16:15.310 --> 00:16:17.840
He apologizes, tries to make things right,

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.240
and genuinely seems to
atone for who he is.

00:16:20.240 --> 00:16:21.240
He appears to ditch

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:23.540
some of the superficial
trappings of his persona,

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:25.750
allowing his hair to gray as he reconciles

00:16:25.750 --> 00:16:27.140
with his former hairdresser

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:28.930
who he got fired from Horsin' Around.

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:30.050
He even tries to get over

00:16:30.050 --> 00:16:32.060
his life-long aversion to other horses.

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:34.110
And then, episode 8.

00:16:34.110 --> 00:16:35.700
Episode 8 is so depressing

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.350
because it reminds us of
the BoJack we used to know.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:41.310
Before the extensive horse
therapy and the apologies.

00:16:41.310 --> 00:16:43.220
Before the even vague sense of decency

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:45.610
or the desire to be a
role model for Hollyhock.

00:16:45.610 --> 00:16:48.280
We're forced to remember
every mistake he made

00:16:48.280 --> 00:16:50.410
and the long list of
victims left in the wake

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:52.430
of his pathological selfishness.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.260
And we're left wondering

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.980
will the changes BoJack
has made even matter?

00:16:56.980 --> 00:16:59.170
Is there a point in your life
when you've hurt enough people

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:02.060
that your own personal
evolution becomes irrelevant?

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:04.380
BoJack's journey has been
one of the most emotional

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.540
of any animated TV protagonist.

00:17:06.540 --> 00:17:09.090
And yet, it's paved entirely with the hurt

00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:13.200
and damaged souls, mostly women,
he's injured along the way.

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:14.560
While the show may be indicating

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:16.670
that BoJack has finally changed,

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:19.410
it also suggests that he
may have caused more damage

00:17:19.410 --> 00:17:22.170
than even the most throughout
evolution could excuse.

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:23.950
So, what does it all mean?

00:17:23.950 --> 00:17:26.100
Well, we think that the
writers of BoJack Horseman

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:27.900
have always situated Horsin' Around

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:29.570
as the imperfect mirror image

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:31.160
to the actual show they're creating.

00:17:31.160 --> 00:17:33.740
Horsin' Around may be
comforting in its platitudes,

00:17:33.740 --> 00:17:35.500
but it's also disingenuous.

00:17:35.500 --> 00:17:36.970
Nobody gets horribly hurt,

00:17:36.970 --> 00:17:39.420
and nobody's particularly deep or honest.

00:17:39.420 --> 00:17:41.380
On BoJack, it's the opposite.

00:17:41.380 --> 00:17:43.570
Everybody is hurting, often horribly.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:44.710
And the characters are often deep

00:17:44.710 --> 00:17:46.610
and quite honest about their pain.

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:47.990
- [Hollyhock] But it wasn't all bad.

00:17:47.990 --> 00:17:51.160
I mean, yeah I got secretly
drugged for months on end

00:17:51.160 --> 00:17:52.590
to the point where I
was living a nightmare

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:54.270
in which I couldn't recognize my own body

00:17:54.270 --> 00:17:56.290
or understand what was
happening to my brain, but

00:17:56.290 --> 00:17:59.520
other than that it was a
pretty chill experience.

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:00.590
- I should've protected you.

00:18:00.590 --> 00:18:02.800
I really wanted to be a
good day to you, Hollyhock.

00:18:02.800 --> 00:18:05.017
- Well, turns out your not my dad at all.

00:18:05.017 --> 00:18:06.905
- Yeah, good thing, I guess.

00:18:06.905 --> 00:18:08.640
- [Helen] The stasis that makes shows

00:18:08.640 --> 00:18:11.550
like Hosin' Around comforting
makes BoJack brilliant,

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:14.240
and sometimes borderline
intolerable to watch.

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:17.240
And we have literally no idea
what's gonna happen next.

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:18.073
Sorry.

00:18:18.073 --> 00:18:19.381
But, what do you guys think?

00:18:19.381 --> 00:18:21.500
Is BoJack beyond redemption?

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:23.990
Is changing oneself
essentially impossible?

00:18:23.990 --> 00:18:27.366
And if so, should any of us
really bother to be better?

00:18:27.366 --> 00:18:29.370
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

